{"name":"Hammam Blue","hex":"#65dcd6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hammam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A communal bathhouse in Islamic countries and communities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hammam"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hammam","blocks":[{"text":"A hammam, also often called a Turkish bath by Westerners, is a type of steam bath or a place of public bathing associated with the Islamic world. It is a prominent feature in the culture of the Muslim world and was inherited from the model of the Roman thermae. Muslim bathhouses or hammams were historically found across the Middle East, North Africa, al-Andalus, Central Asia, the Indian subcontinent, and in Southeastern Europe under Ottoman rule."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Ali_Gholi_Agha_hammam%2C_Isfahan%2C_Iran.jpg/330px-Ali_Gholi_Agha_hammam%2C_Isfahan%2C_Iran.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hammam","wikidata":"Q28077"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG/500px-THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG/1280px-THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG/500px-THERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Remains of the Antonine Baths in ancient Carthage, from the Roman period, in present-day Tunisia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATHERMES_D_ANTONIN_7.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG/500px-20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG/1280px-20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG/500px-20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Qusayr 'Amra in Jordan, among the earliest known examples of Islamic bathhouses, dating from the Umayyad period (7th–8th century)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20100922_qasr_amra14.JPG"},{"srcset":"https